    // 3. Stimulus & self-checking:
    integer errors = 0;
    initial begin
        // Apply stimulus and verify outputs
        // If an output is wrong: errors = errors + 1;
        clk = 0;
        #1 clk = 1;
        if (q != 0) begin
            $display("ERROR: q should be 0, but is %b", q);
            errors = errors + 1;
        end

        clk = 1;
        #1 clk = 0;
        if (q != 0) begin
            $display("ERROR: q should be 0, but is %b", q);
            errors = errors + 1;
        end

        clk = 0;
        #1 clk = 1;
        if (q != 1) begin
            $display("ERROR: q should be 1, but is %b", q);
            errors = errors + 1;
        end

        clk = 1;
        #1 clk = 0;
        if (q != 1) begin
            $display("ERROR: q should be 1, but is %b", q);
            errors = errors + 1;
        end

        // Additional test cases can be added here
        #10;
        if (errors == 0) begin
            $display("*** TEST RESULT: PASS ***");
        end else begin
            $display("*** TEST RESULT: FAIL ***");
        end
        $finish;
    end
endmodule
```

This testbench checks the initial state of the flip-flop (q = 0) and then applies a sequence of clock pulses to see if q correctly transitions to the desired state (q = 1). If any output is incorrect, the testbench increments the `errors` counter and prints an error message. The simulation terminates cleanly with `$finish` after all tests are completed.

